========================================
ARCHITECTURAL FLAW ANALYSIS
========================================
A. Cascading Re-renders:
   Duplicate state ownership across InputField (localValue) and SimpleForm
   (formData, validationErrors, isFormValid, childErrors) forces state changes
   to ping-pong across the parent-child boundary via 5 chained useEffect hooks.
   Note: While 5 chained effect hooks execute, React 19 automatic batching
   combines synchronous state updates within the same effect tick.

B. Race Conditions (MODE A Legacy vs. MODE B Protected):
   - MODE A (Legacy Async Validation): Without request sequence ordering, a slow
     validation request for an early keystroke ("a", 650ms latency) resolves AFTER
     a fast validation request for a later keystroke ("abc", 140ms latency),
     overwriting the valid state with a stale error.
   - MODE B (Protected Validation): Tracks a monotonically increasing request
     sequence ID (seq < latestRaceSeqRef.current) and ignores stale out-of-order
     responses so only the latest user input determines validation state.

C. Infinite Loop Risk (Maximum update depth exceeded):
   When an effect unconditionally creates a new object reference:
     useEffect(() => {
       setFormState({ ...formState });
     });
   or when mutual parent-child effects lack deep-equality guards, every render
   allocates a new reference and schedules another render pass until React throws:
   Uncaught Error: Maximum update depth exceeded.
